From 220070472e741348dbc736c62eac90e2a153847d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sat, 6 Jun 2026 09:30:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] freedreno/a2xx: disable use_comp_lod on TEX_FETCH
 (derivative-LOD bug)

Setting use_comp_lod=1 forces the A2xx fragment shader sampler to
compute LOD from screen-space derivatives of the texture coordinates,
even when the sampler has NEAREST filtering + BASEMAP mip filter
(which together should make any LOD computation irrelevant for
sampling).

For fragment shaders that sample at coordinates with extreme du/dv
across the fragment quad -- e.g. a custom byte-address de-tile
shader for Samsung NV12 input that jumps by 2048 bytes per 64-pixel
tile column on every other fragment -- the implicit-LOD path
produces visibly wrong fetches. Concrete repro: vidc_gl_play on the
HP TouchPad's A220 shows every other 64-pixel tile column offset by
one tile-row (32 px) vertically, even though the GLSL math is
provably correct (CPU de-tile with the same formula on the same
bytes produces a pixel-perfect image).

Disable the computed-LOD path on A2xx; the use_reg_lod path
remains honoured when shaders provide an explicit LOD (src_count
== 2), and the mip filter setting in the sampler view still
controls which level is fetched (BASEMAP -> always level 0).

Note: fragment_shader_texture_lod cap in freedreno_screen.c is
gated to a3xx+ only, so GLES2 shaders on a2xx can't ask for an
explicit LOD via texture2DLodEXT either. This change makes the
implicit fragment-sampling path agree with what NEAREST+BASEMAP
should already be doing.

Upstream-Status: Pending

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../drivers/freedreno/a2xx/ir2_assemble.c     | 26 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/a2xx/ir2_assemble.c b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/a2xx/ir2_assemble.c
index 3b0f7d0bb80..16592786963 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/a2xx/ir2_assemble.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/a2xx/ir2_assemble.c
@@ -201,7 +201,31 @@ fill_instr(struct ir2_context *ctx, struct ir2_sched_instr *sched, instr_t *bc,
          tex->arbitrary_filter = ARBITRARY_FILTER_USE_FETCH_CONST;
          tex->vol_mag_filter = TEX_FILTER_USE_FETCH_CONST;
          tex->vol_min_filter = TEX_FILTER_USE_FETCH_CONST;
-         tex->use_comp_lod = ctx->so->type == MESA_SHADER_FRAGMENT;
+         /*
+          * Setting use_comp_lod=1 forces the A2xx sampler to compute LOD
+          * from screen-space derivatives of the texture coordinates --
+          * even when the sampler has NEAREST filtering + BASEMAP mip
+          * filter (which together should make any LOD computation
+          * irrelevant). For fragment shaders that sample at coordinates
+          * with extreme du/dv across a fragment quad -- e.g. a custom
+          * byte-address de-tile shader for Samsung NV12 input that jumps
+          * by 2048 bytes per 64-pixel tile column -- the implicit-LOD
+          * path produces visibly wrong fetches (every other tile column
+          * shows content offset by one tile-row vertically). Disable
+          * the computed-LOD path; the use_reg_lod path remains honoured
+          * when shaders provide an explicit LOD (src_count == 2) via
+          * the GL_EXT_shader_texture_lod extension, and the mip filter
+          * setting still controls what mip level is actually fetched
+          * (BASEMAP -> always level 0).
+          *
+          * Note: the fragment_shader_texture_lod cap in
+          * src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_screen.c is gated to
+          * a3xx+ only, so GLES2 shaders on a2xx can't request explicit
+          * LOD via texture2DLodEXT either. This change makes the
+          * implicit path agree with the no-mip-no-anisotropic NEAREST
+          * sampling that A2xx is the only architecture able to use.
+          */
+         tex->use_comp_lod = 0;
          tex->use_reg_lod = instr->src_count == 2;
          tex->sample_location = SAMPLE_CENTER;
          tex->tx_coord_denorm = instr->fetch.tex.is_rect;
